# dawn
## Sixteen sous
The bread goes up in the night. It is a thing that a city does as a body does a fever, without anyone having decided it. By the morning bell the loaf in the rue des Noyers is sixteen sous, and there is a woman in the queue who has not got sixteen sous, and a man behind her who has, and between the two of them, on the cobbles, in the gray light, there is nothing at all, which is the trouble.
Baptiste comes in from the street with the news in his hand, as he comes in with everything, wet and breathless and too young for it. It is not the bread. It is something that is being said in the arcades of the Palais Royal, and in the cafés, and in the cabinet makers' shops in the faubourg, in a great many mouths at once. It is said that Monsieur Réveillon, the wallpaper maker of the rue de Montreuil, who has been named to speak at the meeting of the electors of the district, has said that a workman can live very well on fifteen sous a day.
He did not say it. Étienne says so, at the counter, over his spectacles, with the weariness of a man who has known the man for thirty years. He said that a workman might live on fifteen sous, in a good year, if the bread were cheap, and he said it as a thing that he would hope for. It was a Thursday. It is Monday. It is a different sentence now, and it has been said by a thousand people, and it is not going to be un-said.
?if b3.cadets == "sworn"
  There is a lilac ribbon tied to the knocker of the shop door, as there is on a good many doors in the street that you did not know the meaning of. It is only a ribbon. You do not take it off.
?elif b3.cadets == "refused"
  You do not hear from the cellar in the rue de Charonne. It is a silence of a particular kind, and you find that you are listening to it.
?end
?if b3.grain == "hoarded"
  Adélaïde has entered, in her own hand, a figure in the margin of the account book that the house did not have a month ago. She has underlined it once. She does not look at it.
?elif b3.grain == "relieved"
  The chalk board is still in the window. Someone has written under it, in a different hand, in charcoal, a single word. It is *Merci*.
?end
You stand at the window of the counting room with the paper in your hand and the noise of the street coming up, and you know what the next month is going to be, in the way that one knows a coming storm by the stillness of the leaves. It is a very small thing to start from. It is a sentence that a man did not say.
$ do rest()
=> next
